From f6e1b6205f43bb73193e7d0741acc936f808a5e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 23:52:34 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 69/88] xhttp: Close resets the transport instead of ending it

Client.Close marked its pools closed for good, and every dial after it failed with "use
of closed network connection". The outbounds call Close of their transport on every
network reset (InterfaceUpdated: a change of the default interface, its first appearance
after a start without one, "close all connections" of the Clash API) and go on dialing
through the same transport, as every other V2Ray transport allows. An xhttp outbound was
dead from the first such event until a restart.

Close is a reset now. It closes the pooled connections, the retired ones and the QUIC
connections of HTTP/3 as before, for the upload pool and for a separate download pool,
and the sessions open on them fail through them; the next dial opens a new connection.
The failures, the backoff and the last error of the pool are forgotten with the network
they were seen on. The connections a reset closed belong to an earlier generation of the
pool: the errors their requests report afterwards do not count against the server, so
they cannot start a backoff of the new connections, and such a client is not listed as
retired again.

What ends the transport is the context it was created with: once that is done the pool
opens no connections, so the Close of an outbound that shuts down leaves nothing behind
whatever dials race with it (sing-box cancels the context first).

release looks at the removed mark under the lock of the idle timer, so that a reset
between the two cannot leave a timer running on a closed client.

A client that was shut down refuses further requests itself. A session that outlived a
reset and sent one more upload through the client it held made the HTTP client dial and
handshake a new connection, only to have it closed at once.

Tests: a reset with sessions open, over HTTP/2, HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/3, in every mode, with
a shared and with a separate download pool; failures reported after a reset; resets
during dials; a dial after the context is done; a request through a closed client.
